WEBVTT

00:00:02.590 --> 00:00:21.100
 It's called soil and shadow by focusing on soil as a nexus between food systems and climate change work, and specifically looking at the carbon that can be drawn out of the atmosphere and put into the soil, and having that be a deep partner to the reducing carbon emissions work that I've always done in the climate world.

00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:31.180
 There's a way that that creates livelihoods and builds political and economic bridges between different types of community if we do regional economic development.

00:00:31.180 --> 00:00:34.370
 And so that's the big dream on the soil side.

00:00:34.850 --> 00:00:52.520
 And the shadow side is lifting up the fact that anything that's hidden and boiling under the surface, as we started talking about when you asked about the political situation, it's just acknowledging that our inner world must be attended to as we do this work of social change.

00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:59.960
 Because if they don't get attended to, they're going to come out in insidious ways that are going to break apart the beautiful things we're trying to build.

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:17.600
 Welcome to Delicious Revolution, a show about food, culture, and place. I'm Chelsea Wills.

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:23.600
 This season, we're talking with visionary chefs, gardeners, farmers, organizers, artists, and scientists.

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:26.820
 These people have shaped the food movement in California.

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:37.110
 We talk with a diverse group of California's rebel food makers about the ways they do things in their farms, kitchens, and communities that reshape the way we think about food.

00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:40.110
 This show is made by Devin Sampson and Chelsea Wills.

00:01:40.110 --> 00:01:47.110
 Special thanks to the support from Cal Humanities, Food First, and Rebecca Murillo for making this season possible.

00:01:47.110 --> 00:01:53.250
 Nikki Silvestri is an advocate for climate solutions, healthy food systems, and social change.

00:01:53.250 --> 00:02:08.720
 As the co-founder of Live Real and former executive director for People's Grocery and Green for All, Nikki has built and strengthened social equity for underrepresented populations in food systems, social services, public health, climate solutions, and economic development.

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:15.170
 Her many honors include being named one of the Root's 100 most influential African Americans in 2014.

00:02:15.170 --> 00:02:24.520
 Nikki is co-founder and CEO of Soil and Shadow, a project and design management firm working on supporting thriving communities, economies, and natural environments.

00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:31.420
 Here's my interview with Nikki Silvestri.

00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:34.420
 All right, Nikki, thanks so much for joining us on Delicious Revolution.

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:35.640
 Thank you for having me.

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:37.640
 I have a lot to ask you about.

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:40.640
 So can I start with a hard question for you?

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:41.640
 Sure can.

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:42.640
 I'm ready.

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:43.640
 I think it's a hard question.

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:48.660
 Of course, I've felt a lot of things about, you know, the first week and a half or so of the Trump presidency.

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:58.660
 But one thing I can't quite wrap my head around is what that means for those of us who have done a lot of our activism and thinking and work about food and the food movement.

00:02:59.660 --> 00:03:04.040
 How are you thinking about this and then the new kind of political landscape that we're working in?

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:05.810
 Food.

00:03:05.810 --> 00:03:17.310
 I will start with an overall comment on how I'm framing these times, which will help when it comes to how I think about food and food systems specifically.

00:03:17.310 --> 00:03:27.030
 A good metaphor for what's happening right now feels like we had a bunch of broken bones that healed in the wrong way.

00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:36.000
 And we were hobbling along with those incorrectly healed bones for a while and just got into a car accident.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:38.260
 Well, now there's the opportunity.

00:03:38.260 --> 00:03:45.430
 Some of the bones that were already broken and healed the wrong way rebroke and a lot of them didn't.

00:03:45.430 --> 00:03:54.220
 And so now we're at that place of are we going to actually rebreak everything and put ourselves back together or not?

00:03:54.220 --> 00:03:55.420
 Right.

00:03:55.420 --> 00:04:04.570
 We feel like we're in a state of collective trauma in a way because unearthing things that have been dormant for so long is very painful.

00:04:04.570 --> 00:04:09.150
 And so that's a bit of the way that I'm engaging when it comes to the food system.

00:04:09.150 --> 00:04:23.630
 One of the things that was under the surface and was hidden was the way that our GDP is it comes from the top percentage of counties in the same way that wealth concentration is the top percentage of people.

00:04:23.630 --> 00:04:29.230
 And most of the counties in the United States are very poor and very under invested.

00:04:29.230 --> 00:04:33.340
 And they're not necessarily when it comes to equity.

00:04:33.340 --> 00:04:44.890
 They don't look like the urban people of color necessarily across the board that I think a lot of social equity activists consider to be the main focus of equity work.

00:04:44.890 --> 00:04:52.800
 And so with my work with soil, I had to go through that journey myself, you know, not to actually generalize with any other activists.

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:58.380
 But I feel like my food story was about urban people of color, food insecurity.

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:12.190
 And the more work that I've done in soil, the more I can see how bridging the urban rural divide, if that's not central to my work, then I'm not approaching social equity in a way that can actually lift us all up together.

00:05:12.190 --> 00:05:28.470
 So I think there's enormous opportunity in the food movement to look at the fact that the people who spoke during this election are the people in rural communities who have been disinvested in over time.

00:05:28.470 --> 00:05:36.930
 And that agriculture and ranching and food systems work is one of the main ways to reconnect with them in a way that's healthy and economically viable.

00:05:36.930 --> 00:05:43.830
 It's just moving across cultural divides is going to be the big challenge, probably.

00:05:43.830 --> 00:05:53.230
 I think I probably should back up a little bit and talk about what you've been doing over the last several years.

00:05:53.230 --> 00:05:56.230
 So you were the co-founder of Live Real.

00:05:56.230 --> 00:06:01.230
 You were the executive director for a while of People's Grocery and then Green for All.

00:06:01.230 --> 00:06:09.230
 So you've done a lot of your work in kind of the nexus of social equity and economic development.

00:06:09.230 --> 00:06:10.230
 Mm-hmm.

00:06:10.230 --> 00:06:14.230
 So those are some of the big questions that are politically on the table now.

00:06:14.230 --> 00:06:17.220
 Can you tell me how you started thinking about that?

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:25.750
 Well, my parents run a foster family agency in Los Angeles, and I grew up kind of watching them work in the social services sector.

00:06:25.750 --> 00:06:26.750
 Mm-hmm.

00:06:26.750 --> 00:06:31.750
 And I think that that was probably the first place that the nexus of economic development and social equity happened for me.

00:06:31.750 --> 00:06:32.750
 Mm-hmm.

00:06:32.750 --> 00:06:38.320
 Because livelihoods, broken families and broken livelihoods tend to go hand in hand.

00:06:38.320 --> 00:06:51.450
 And I saw very early that for a family to be able to focus on loving each other and being a thriving family, livelihood has to be taken care of.

00:06:51.450 --> 00:06:51.550
 Mm-hmm.

00:06:51.450 --> 00:06:54.750
 Money is one of the main things people fight about.

00:06:54.750 --> 00:06:58.600
 And so they never were divorced in my mind.

00:06:58.600 --> 00:06:59.580
 Right, right.

00:06:59.580 --> 00:07:02.240
 Can you tell me about Live Real?

00:07:02.240 --> 00:07:03.430
 Yeah.

00:07:03.430 --> 00:07:10.750
 I became interested in sustainability in college, having been interested in environmentalism since I was a little kid.

00:07:10.750 --> 00:07:17.250
 And the concept of sustainability just really struck me and continues to strike me when it comes to the triple bottom line.

00:07:17.250 --> 00:07:32.090
 And Live Real came out of a conversation between me and four other folk that I got to know during my extracurricular food activism when I was working on green jobs about what's going to be next for the youth food movement.

00:07:32.090 --> 00:07:40.420
 And it's since morphed and changed and become a bunch of different things and was really deeply rooted in the real food challenge and a lot of Novena Khanna's work.

00:07:40.420 --> 00:07:55.130
 And so that group of people was just deeply inspiring to me when it came to how we're going to build the next wave of the food movement with the young people right now that are doing the good work.

00:07:55.130 --> 00:07:58.980
 And did you go from there to people's grocery?

00:07:58.980 --> 00:08:02.010
 Well, Live Real was, it was an extracurricular activity.

00:08:02.010 --> 00:08:03.010
 Okay, yeah, yeah.

00:08:03.010 --> 00:08:33.330
 Yeah, yeah.

00:08:33.330 --> 00:08:50.220
 And really just increasing the velocity of the activities in West Oakland that were already happening and providing whatever support we could to allow for community to build in the way that it naturally does when it's not artificially stopped from doing so.

00:08:50.220 --> 00:09:19.010
 Yeah, so let's talk a little bit about West Oakland because you came there and what felt to me like a really exciting time, both in terms of what people were doing there in food activism, but also kind of the concepts that were coming out about how people were talking about, I don't know, drawing on some old ideas and some of the self-reliance ideas of the Black Panthers, but also a lot of organizing about how to make a food system work in an urban place.

00:09:19.010 --> 00:09:23.980
 What was happening there when you got there and what were you guys working on?

00:09:23.980 --> 00:09:25.580
 What were you guys working on?

00:09:25.580 --> 00:09:40.230
 I feel pretty humble now about my time in people's grocery because there's a way that West Oakland is such a, it has such a long arc of being a holding ground for transition.

00:09:40.230 --> 00:09:59.850
 You know, from being an Eastern European community before the Great Migration out of the South when the train station was still there, and then being a predominantly African American community that was the hotbed of so much activism and small business, thriving small business until the freeways and the BART station came in.

00:09:59.850 --> 00:10:11.220
 And everything that we were doing at People's Grocery, so many of the things we were supporting people to do had already been tried 20, sometimes even 30 years before.

00:10:11.220 --> 00:10:21.380
 And so we really were just attempting to continue the momentum that had these different starts and stops based on what economic development policies were happening around them.

00:10:21.380 --> 00:10:26.340
 And really reflecting that mirror back to people is there's nothing inherently wrong with this community.

00:10:26.340 --> 00:10:43.210
 It's that this is why systemic disinvestment is so insidious, because it can create the appearance of decades worth of families that are broken and police brutality and low income housing, etc.

00:10:43.210 --> 00:10:46.170
 All of the things that come with the disinvested communities.

00:10:46.170 --> 00:10:59.420
 But to look under the surface at the self-determination activities that are always happening underneath that, West Oakland is just, it's one of the best examples to me of how a community just keeps going.

00:10:59.420 --> 00:11:07.860
 And we as the nonprofit, we're trying to be part of the infrastructure to support that drive to keep going, no matter what was happening systemically around them.

00:11:07.860 --> 00:11:18.300
 And that showed up as community gardens and a community supported agriculture produce box and a micro business incubation program partnership with a public hospital, etc.

00:11:19.270 --> 00:11:22.300
 What year was it that you that you went to People's Grocery?

00:11:22.300 --> 00:11:23.430
 January of 2010.

00:11:23.430 --> 00:11:24.430
 Of 2010.

00:11:24.430 --> 00:11:27.430
 Yeah, I guess what was what was happening around that community then?

00:11:27.430 --> 00:11:35.280
 Was it kind of a period of disinvestment or had the kind of buy up of people's houses and land started by then?

00:11:35.280 --> 00:11:41.090
 There's a lot of property changing hands right now.

00:11:41.090 --> 00:11:45.120
 Yeah, that was not happening when I got to People's Grocery in 2010.

00:11:45.120 --> 00:11:50.150
 So things have changed in the last four years that are in a pretty dramatic way.

00:11:50.150 --> 00:11:55.780
 When we got there, I feel like the the train was continuing to roll with everything I said before.

00:11:55.780 --> 00:11:58.690
 There weren't any circumstances that I would lift up in particular.

00:11:58.690 --> 00:12:00.740
 What did you do after People's Grocery?

00:12:00.740 --> 00:12:02.740
 Were you working for Green for All again?

00:12:02.740 --> 00:12:03.990
 Mm hmm.

00:12:03.990 --> 00:12:05.990
 I became the executive director of Green for All.

00:12:05.990 --> 00:12:06.990
 Green for All.

00:12:06.990 --> 00:12:07.090
 Yeah.

00:12:06.990 --> 00:12:07.990
 And what did you do there?

00:12:07.990 --> 00:12:08.990
 Big picture.

00:12:08.990 --> 00:12:10.990
 It was about supporting the president's climate action plan.

00:12:10.990 --> 00:12:13.990
 That that was the big unique thing that was happening in 2014.

00:12:13.990 --> 00:12:19.820
 That we needed to make sure that African-Americans across the country knew that the clean power

00:12:19.820 --> 00:12:25.010
 plan and that having a president that was actually willing to put limits on carbon was a really good

00:12:25.010 --> 00:12:26.130
 thing and to support it.

00:12:26.130 --> 00:12:32.760
 And so there were events around that and a lot of policy work in D.C. around that and organizing

00:12:32.760 --> 00:12:33.760
 and advocacy around that.

00:12:33.760 --> 00:12:40.850
 And then being a predominantly African-American community that was the hotbed of so much activism.

00:12:40.850 --> 00:12:46.010
 You guys were talking a lot about economic development around this transition to clean energy.

00:12:46.010 --> 00:12:47.010
 And small business, thriving small business until the freeways.

00:12:47.010 --> 00:12:49.010
 Is that most of the organizing that you were doing?

00:12:49.010 --> 00:12:50.300
 Well, yeah.

00:12:50.300 --> 00:12:55.190
 I mean, Green for All overall was always, always looking at green jobs.

00:12:55.190 --> 00:12:59.220
 And that was just the clean power plan was just one thread.

00:12:59.220 --> 00:13:04.630
 But the organization was kind of an octopus when it came to doing policy work and on the ground

00:13:04.630 --> 00:13:08.980
 demonstration projects for what workforce development programs can look like when they're focused

00:13:08.980 --> 00:13:09.980
 on green jobs.

00:13:09.980 --> 00:13:15.320
 And then supporting advocates and activists across the country in the fellowship program

00:13:15.320 --> 00:13:19.200
 and a business incubation support program.

00:13:19.200 --> 00:13:25.320
 There were a bunch of things after this is why systemic disinvestment is so insidious because

00:13:25.320 --> 00:13:28.320
 it can create the appearance of really cool projects.

00:13:28.320 --> 00:13:36.320
 Decades worth of the families that are broken and police brutality and low income housing,

00:13:36.320 --> 00:13:37.320
 et cetera.

00:13:37.320 --> 00:13:39.320
 All the things that come with the disinvested community.

00:13:39.320 --> 00:13:40.510
 Yeah.

00:13:40.510 --> 00:13:44.610
 So it's good that we're doing this interview now because I'm in the process of rebranding

00:13:44.610 --> 00:13:49.050
 my firm and called soil and shadow now.

00:13:49.050 --> 00:13:59.440
 And the overarching theme there is that by focusing on soil as a nexus between food systems and climate

00:13:59.440 --> 00:14:04.600
 change work and specifically looking at the carbon that can be drawn out of the atmosphere

00:14:04.600 --> 00:14:09.920
 and put into the soil and having that be a deep partner to the reducing carbon emissions

00:14:09.920 --> 00:14:11.920
 work that I've always done in the climate world.

00:14:11.920 --> 00:14:18.280
 There's a way that that creates livelihoods and builds political and economic bridges between

00:14:18.280 --> 00:14:21.280
 different types of community if we do regional economic development.

00:14:21.280 --> 00:14:22.280
 Huh.

00:14:22.280 --> 00:14:24.280
 And so that's the big dream on the soil side.

00:14:24.280 --> 00:14:25.280
 Sure, yeah.

00:14:25.280 --> 00:14:30.700
 And the shadow side is lifting up the fact that anything that's hidden and boiling under

00:14:30.700 --> 00:14:34.830
 the surface, as we started talking about when you asked about the political situation.

00:14:34.830 --> 00:14:35.830
 Yeah.

00:14:35.830 --> 00:14:41.880
 It's just acknowledging that our inner worlds must be attended to as we do this work of social

00:14:41.880 --> 00:14:42.880
 change.

00:14:42.880 --> 00:14:47.070
 Because if they don't get attended to, they're going to come out in insidious ways that are

00:14:47.070 --> 00:14:50.380
 going to break apart the beautiful things we're trying to build.

00:14:50.380 --> 00:14:51.380
 Yeah, yeah, sure.

00:14:51.380 --> 00:14:52.990
 Yeah.

00:14:52.990 --> 00:14:57.210
 Well, I definitely want to talk more about the shadow part, but I think I want to start with

00:14:57.210 --> 00:14:58.210
 the soil part.

00:14:58.210 --> 00:15:04.210
 And can you elaborate a little bit about why you see so much potential in the soil?

00:15:04.210 --> 00:15:05.210
 Yeah.

00:15:05.210 --> 00:15:15.480
 So I think that the concept of working landscapes is one that's been really up for me.

00:15:15.480 --> 00:15:23.920
 That there's a way that the mainstream environmental movement still kind of looks at pristine land

00:15:23.920 --> 00:15:27.280
 as being untouched and should stay untouched.

00:15:27.280 --> 00:15:32.610
 And there's a lot of land that really should be and should just be left alone because it's

00:15:32.610 --> 00:15:33.920
 beautiful.

00:15:33.920 --> 00:15:41.030
 But there's a lot of land that's working landscape and needs to be cultivated in partnership with

00:15:41.030 --> 00:15:42.030
 humans and animals.

00:15:42.030 --> 00:15:47.810
 And the divide between the way that we treat pristine, quote unquote, land and, quote, working

00:15:47.810 --> 00:15:51.260
 land is just really, it's very stark.

00:15:51.260 --> 00:15:53.830
 Never the two shall meet, really.

00:15:53.830 --> 00:15:56.800
 And those communities actually tend to be in conflict a lot.

00:15:56.800 --> 00:16:06.760
 And so my view on soil is that there's a middle ground where land can be regenerated as a working

00:16:06.760 --> 00:16:08.050
 landscape.

00:16:08.050 --> 00:16:11.050
 And regenerative agriculture has been doing this forever.

00:16:11.050 --> 00:16:12.050
 So this is not a new concept.

00:16:12.050 --> 00:16:17.400
 But I think the potential for me of looking at soil is the scale, that there's a way that

00:16:17.400 --> 00:16:21.400
 mainstream conservation and mainstream agriculture, they're dealing with such, you know, millions

00:16:21.400 --> 00:16:23.400
 of acres in a lot of cases.

00:16:23.400 --> 00:16:26.620
 It's just such a massive scale.

00:16:26.620 --> 00:16:30.730
 And we need to think of that scale when it comes to soil and working landscapes and how

00:16:30.730 --> 00:16:35.990
 businesses can be created that are regional, that help support local economies, and how it

00:16:35.990 --> 00:16:39.650
 can contribute to the health of the carbon cycle.

00:16:39.650 --> 00:16:47.490
 Because that's something that carbon sequestration is very technical and a bit wonky and is also

00:16:47.490 --> 00:16:49.550
 really complicated.

00:16:49.550 --> 00:16:53.550
 That, you know, there's glitches like when you put a lot of carbon in the soil, then a lot

00:16:53.550 --> 00:16:56.550
 more carbon is going to start coming out of the ocean, et cetera.

00:16:56.550 --> 00:16:59.700
 And so there's a lot of debate in the climate community about how much of a solution, quote

00:16:59.700 --> 00:17:03.820
 unquote, sequestering carbon is to climate change.

00:17:03.820 --> 00:17:09.190
 But for me, the point is more making sure that the carbon that is cycling is cycling at

00:17:09.190 --> 00:17:12.250
 a fast enough rate to be healthy for human life.

00:17:12.250 --> 00:17:15.410
 And right now, there's just a lot of carbon that's stuck.

00:17:15.410 --> 00:17:16.410
 It's stuck in the ocean.

00:17:16.410 --> 00:17:18.410
 It's stuck in the atmosphere.

00:17:18.410 --> 00:17:23.660
 There's soil that can't capture anything or cycle anything because it's almost dead.

00:17:23.660 --> 00:17:28.220
 And so it's creating health around the cycle of carbon.

00:17:28.220 --> 00:17:29.230
 And there's a few.

00:17:29.230 --> 00:17:33.350
 A lot of my work this year between research papers and working with philanthropists and

00:17:33.350 --> 00:17:39.110
 investors has been about lifting up the stories of the people that are doing that regenerative

00:17:39.110 --> 00:17:45.350
 work that could be at scale and really lifting up regional solutions that need to be invested

00:17:45.350 --> 00:17:52.080
 in, especially as we go into a new administration, that, you know, the best of which should be

00:17:52.080 --> 00:17:57.200
 focused on recapturing wealth for Americans that have not had it.

00:17:57.200 --> 00:17:58.380
 Mm hmm.

00:17:58.380 --> 00:18:02.380
 I guess I either want to talk, get more specific about some of those projects, or maybe you'd

00:18:02.380 --> 00:18:07.950
 prefer to just talk about like what, what kind of project you're hoping to do, or like what

00:18:07.950 --> 00:18:12.050
 would be a dream project in soil and who would you work with?

00:18:12.050 --> 00:18:19.150
 A dream would be working on demonstration projects that accelerate collaboration.

00:18:19.150 --> 00:18:20.150
 That collaboration is really hard.

00:18:20.150 --> 00:18:25.360
 I want to see soil projects that are bridging urban and rural communities where there are

00:18:25.360 --> 00:18:28.360
 ranchers who are trying to figure out how to work with inner city food and security communities

00:18:28.360 --> 00:18:34.670
 that are thinking big picture about their entire state and how their state's climate plan

00:18:34.670 --> 00:18:39.670
 relates to their work in a way that's also building wealth for both rural and inner city communities,

00:18:39.670 --> 00:18:44.990
 things like that. And there probably are projects like that happening already, which is why my

00:18:44.990 --> 00:18:49.180
 firm focuses on accelerating things that are already happening, because a lot of people have

00:18:49.180 --> 00:18:54.790
 a lot of really good ideas, but they don't know how to operationalize those. And so that's

00:18:54.790 --> 00:19:00.900
 what we do is we help with operationalizing through research and through other types of

00:19:00.900 --> 00:19:01.900
 collaboration.

00:19:01.900 --> 00:19:08.960
 Yeah, collaboration is, is hard. It's, um, I've often become frustrated with talking to rural

00:19:08.960 --> 00:19:14.120
 folks who will say like, yeah, we just, people don't know where their food comes from anymore.

00:19:14.120 --> 00:19:18.280
 We kind of got to teach them. And then talking to urban folks who say like these farmers, they

00:19:18.280 --> 00:19:23.280
 got to get with the program and, uh, you know, sort out their, their commitment to actually

00:19:23.280 --> 00:19:28.560
 feeding people and their issues with racism and that kind of thing. And, uh, and, but this, but

00:19:28.560 --> 00:19:34.200
 this, it's easy to say those things, but the part where you come together is, is, is difficult.

00:19:34.200 --> 00:19:39.230
 Um, how do we do it? What, what are some of the approaches that you use to help make those

00:19:39.230 --> 00:19:40.230
 collaborations work?

00:19:40.230 --> 00:19:43.960
 That gets into the shadow side of your work.

00:19:43.960 --> 00:19:44.960
 Yeah. All right. Good.

00:19:44.960 --> 00:19:51.210
 So, um, there's a firm that I work with called Tenth Dot Consulting, and it's a mix of yogic

00:19:51.210 --> 00:19:56.210
 traditions, gestalt organizational development, and a bunch of other modalities. And really,

00:19:56.210 --> 00:20:02.420
 no matter where one starts, there are tools for how to do this in psychology and organizational

00:20:02.420 --> 00:20:09.950
 development work in systems theory in, you know, ancient Eastern texts. But the common thread

00:20:09.950 --> 00:20:21.040
 is projection and it's not you, it's me, which is the hardest thing. It's so easy when we experience

00:20:21.040 --> 00:20:27.450
 discomfort as humans to externalize that discomfort elsewhere, instead of seeing how we're the ones

00:20:27.450 --> 00:20:33.860
 causing ourselves to have a certain experience and be in relationship with that. So the tools I use

00:20:33.860 --> 00:20:40.010
 tend to be on the gestalt slash yogic side, but there are a bunch of other tools to help with that.

00:20:40.010 --> 00:20:48.050
 And I think that that first question of what does it take to see that it's not you, it's me,

00:20:48.050 --> 00:20:53.050
 the dynamic that you just mentioned between urban people and rural people was all about them pointing

00:20:53.050 --> 00:20:56.020
 at the other person and lifting up their shit.

00:20:56.020 --> 00:21:05.160
 And now I feel like it's time to look home first and really have a humble approach to the way that

00:21:05.160 --> 00:21:12.470
 we do our own work and how we relate to other people and have places to practice that knowing

00:21:12.470 --> 00:21:16.680
 that it's probably going to go, interestingly, the first few times we try it.

00:21:16.680 --> 00:21:23.060
 I think last time we talked, you said something that I remember and turned over

00:21:23.060 --> 00:21:27.120
 and over in my mind. We were talking about the food movement. And I think I asked you

00:21:27.120 --> 00:21:31.120
 like whether or not you kind of saw yourself as part of it. And one of the things that you said is like

00:21:31.120 --> 00:21:37.910
 the food movement really needs to look at some of the elephants in the room. Do you remember talking about that?

00:21:37.910 --> 00:21:39.580
 Kind of.

00:21:39.580 --> 00:21:40.580
 Kind of.

00:21:40.580 --> 00:21:46.580
 Kind of. Because I wanted to ask you again what the elephants are in the room of the food movement.

00:21:46.580 --> 00:21:50.670
 Well, what's funny is that between last time and now lots change.

00:21:50.670 --> 00:21:51.670
 Right.

00:21:51.670 --> 00:21:59.670
 So I feel like the one of the biggest elephants in the room is politics along the lines of the food movement

00:21:59.670 --> 00:22:02.670
 and the food system are currently fairly divided.

00:22:02.670 --> 00:22:03.670
 Sure.

00:22:03.670 --> 00:22:07.670
 And a lot of people working in the food system don't have the politics of progressives who feel like

00:22:07.670 --> 00:22:12.060
 they're a part of the food movement. And we've got to look at that.

00:22:12.060 --> 00:22:12.160
 Right.

00:22:12.060 --> 00:22:14.550
 And we've got to figure that out.

00:22:14.550 --> 00:22:19.840
 Because seven out of the 10 lowest paying jobs in the country are food systems jobs.

00:22:19.840 --> 00:22:25.560
 And a lot of those people are really just trying to make sure that their kids get fed

00:22:25.560 --> 00:22:30.040
 and make sure that their families are healthy and just make it day to day.

00:22:30.040 --> 00:22:35.000
 It's not actually as complicated as us activists would like to think it is.

00:22:35.000 --> 00:22:47.520
 And so being able to be in relationship in a simple way and not see simplicity as being ignorance is something that I think the food movement really needs to take on.

00:22:47.520 --> 00:22:50.900
 What does that mean to be in relationship in a simple way?

00:22:50.900 --> 00:23:13.780
 I think just not devaluing other people's experience and understanding the prism of how people carry their story and having enough self-stability to be able to see all of what someone is and then let case by case determine how one is in relationship to them.

00:23:13.780 --> 00:23:21.250
 So for example, right. And this is a very personal example that I would never, ever encourage someone else to do if it doesn't feel right.

00:23:21.250 --> 00:23:29.270
 But there are people I've interacted with who don't think very highly of black people at all and use the N word to describe us.

00:23:29.270 --> 00:23:42.690
 But they also have a very southern, genteel kind of, are the most polite people I've ever met, would never, ever be rude to me in their home.

00:23:42.690 --> 00:23:45.130
 And they're incredibly hospitable.

00:23:45.130 --> 00:23:49.970
 They just have interesting views on black folk.

00:23:49.970 --> 00:23:56.770
 I feel like identity politics can get in the way of just being able to relate on some basic stuff.

00:23:56.770 --> 00:24:01.520
 And that's the kind of humility that I feel like I'm walking around the world with now as a leader.

00:24:01.520 --> 00:24:07.740
 A lot of these things that I'm saying that probably feel like they're directed at other people, you know, it's not you, it's me.

00:24:07.740 --> 00:24:16.400
 Like it's me talking to myself around the way that I observed my own leadership over the years and how high and mighty I actually was without realizing it.

00:24:16.400 --> 00:24:20.740
 And how humble I've gotten around how much of this country I have no idea about.

00:24:20.740 --> 00:24:22.990
 And I'm not talking about the extremes of it.

00:24:22.990 --> 00:24:29.950
 You know, there are absolutely some people I would never be in the same room with because there is actual danger to my emotional or physical health.

00:24:29.950 --> 00:24:32.170
 I'm not talking about them.

00:24:32.170 --> 00:24:42.570
 I'm talking about, you know, the other 70% of people that are somewhere in the middle that we may have discomfort around each other, but we're not going to be unsafe around each other.

00:24:42.570 --> 00:24:50.140
 And what kind of self-stability does it take for me to be able to walk through that discomfort into relationship knowing that I'm going to be challenged?

00:24:50.140 --> 00:24:54.090
 What's to be gained from that kind of relationship?

00:24:54.090 --> 00:24:56.090
 What promise does that hold?

00:24:56.090 --> 00:25:03.270
 Well, for me, those relationships are the thing that provide the foundation for big picture systemic solutions.

00:25:03.270 --> 00:25:13.350
 You know, that's where a lot of regional stuff doesn't work because urban and rural communities are so fundamentally different that decision making between them is hard.

00:25:13.350 --> 00:25:20.490
 And those kind of relationships can ensure that regional decisions can actually be made in a way that's clear and legitimate.

00:25:20.490 --> 00:25:36.780
 When you think about this, are you thinking about some specific decisions that need to be made or are you talking more about kind of a creative process here of coming up with some solutions to some big problems or both?

00:25:36.780 --> 00:25:38.000
 I think both.

00:25:38.000 --> 00:25:46.430
 I mean, really specifically, you know, there's there's money at the state level that's earmarked for or at least I'll just I'll talk about California.

00:25:46.430 --> 00:25:57.530
 There's a lot of natural resource management money that's earmarked and a lot of the way people think about natural resource management is not in urban communities because you think about natural resources as being not urban.

00:25:57.530 --> 00:26:05.900
 And just having a regional approach to that and looking at water systems and how they connect between urban and rural communities and the job creation possibilities there.

00:26:05.900 --> 00:26:15.120
 I'll say specifically when it comes to soil, I was pretty surprised at the lack of connection between the fact that soil doesn't start and stop.

00:26:15.120 --> 00:26:18.120
 It just keeps going, even if it's under concrete.

00:26:18.120 --> 00:26:25.080
 And there's a way to look at an entire region when it comes to a healthy soils plan.

00:26:25.080 --> 00:26:32.740
 But that's it's it's a difficult thing when ecosystems aren't looked at at large.

00:26:32.740 --> 00:26:41.460
 And so there's agroecology understanding, there's ecosystems understanding, there's the way that natural resource management is community building understanding.

00:26:41.460 --> 00:26:46.650
 There's a bunch of different understandings that we're looking to seek common ground on.

00:26:46.650 --> 00:26:51.790
 And a democratic process is one of the first places that starts.

00:26:51.790 --> 00:26:52.790
 Right, right.

00:26:52.790 --> 00:26:58.790
 There's an interview that I did for this season also is Ron Reed, who's a he's part of the Karuk tribe.

00:26:58.790 --> 00:27:10.670
 And he talks about sitting on these panels of the US Forest Service and talking about how his people have traditionally managed the forest in question.

00:27:10.670 --> 00:27:31.630
 And him talking about concepts like sacred fire and how that started with just blank stares and just how difficult it was to sit through meeting after meeting after meeting until there's, you know, years into the process, there started to be some common understanding and they're doing some really innovative stuff.

00:27:31.630 --> 00:27:44.800
 So, yeah, I know. I mean, I really he talks a lot about just how uncomfortable that is, that process of these unlikely and uncomfortable alliances that are necessary for to try something new.

00:27:44.800 --> 00:27:46.980
 That's exactly what I'm talking about.

00:27:46.980 --> 00:27:47.980
 Is that what you're talking about?

00:27:47.980 --> 00:27:48.980
 Is that the kind of.

00:27:48.980 --> 00:27:58.150
 So you're stepping into a lot of these tension filled places where where there's a lot of promise for for something big to happen.

00:27:58.150 --> 00:28:09.600
 Yes. And looking at where your example is perfect, because those are the kind of things that happening all over the place and being able to see that as a bigger story.

00:28:09.600 --> 00:28:18.690
 Telling the big story feels really important for me in 2017, because a lot of what's happening right now in terms of storytelling is around the extremes.

00:28:18.690 --> 00:28:24.780
 And people are starting to think that the extremes are the normal and the extremes are not the norm.

00:28:24.780 --> 00:28:28.000
 What you're describing is actually the norm that's happening all over the place.

00:28:28.000 --> 00:28:33.980
 They're just not they're not sexy enough to lift up because it's actually the good version of the story.

00:28:33.980 --> 00:28:39.940
 Well, Chelsea and I really we think a lot about storytelling and the role that has in movement.

00:28:39.940 --> 00:28:44.940
 And one of the reasons that I really wanted to talk to you was because, you know, I was doing research on a bunch of people.

00:28:44.940 --> 00:28:46.940
 I started reading your blog.

00:28:46.940 --> 00:28:49.890
 You know, I wasn't expecting something poetic.

00:28:49.890 --> 00:28:52.930
 I mean, not a lot of policy people.

00:28:52.930 --> 00:28:53.930
 Right.

00:28:53.930 --> 00:28:55.210
 And in such a poetic way.

00:28:55.210 --> 00:28:59.210
 And I pulled out a quote where you're for just as an example, you're talking about.

00:28:59.210 --> 00:29:04.230
 We shouldn't treat the natural environment as something out there that we can choose to either ignore and protect.

00:29:04.230 --> 00:29:09.230
 And then you get into this thing about how we relate to these big issues on a poetic level.

00:29:09.230 --> 00:29:13.480
 There's something really compelling about a story like that.

00:29:13.480 --> 00:29:23.540
 And and I'm so intrigued by the idea that that that's an approach that we can use even in this political climate.

00:29:23.540 --> 00:29:26.530
 That seems totally, you know, totally crazy.

00:29:26.530 --> 00:29:30.290
 So what what makes a good story?

00:29:30.290 --> 00:29:32.290
 How do we tell stories to build movement?

00:29:32.290 --> 00:29:37.880
 And how do we tell stories that hold that kind of promise?

00:29:37.880 --> 00:29:40.940
 That feels like the question right now.

00:29:40.940 --> 00:29:41.940
 Yeah.

00:29:41.940 --> 00:29:44.940
 Especially because post truth, I think, was added to the dictionary.

00:29:44.940 --> 00:29:49.390
 And we are in a conversation about alternative facts.

00:29:49.390 --> 00:29:50.390
 Yeah.

00:29:50.390 --> 00:29:51.350
 And like that.

00:29:51.350 --> 00:29:55.490
 And that's a great example of it's not you.

00:29:55.490 --> 00:29:56.490
 It's me.

00:29:56.490 --> 00:29:57.660
 Sure.

00:29:57.660 --> 00:30:04.150
 What would it take for me to understand the need for alternative facts and where that comes from?

00:30:04.150 --> 00:30:20.480
 And the process I've gone through with that is that there are people who have not seen themselves as a part of the story who have felt who have felt unseen, unacknowledged and not supported.

00:30:20.480 --> 00:30:27.780
 And there's a difference between addressing a pain point and feeling like the pain point has been addressed.

00:30:27.780 --> 00:30:29.880
 Those are like worlds apart.

00:30:29.880 --> 00:30:30.880
 Yeah.

00:30:30.880 --> 00:30:32.880
 And I think that that's what we're learning right now.

00:30:32.880 --> 00:30:38.190
 And so the story for me addresses the feeling of the pain point having been addressed.

00:30:38.190 --> 00:30:39.170
 We're whole people.

00:30:39.170 --> 00:30:46.290
 We're not just a paycheck, which is why the difference between livelihoods and jobs feels so important to me.

00:30:46.290 --> 00:30:51.950
 Livelihoods means community building and craftsmanship and legacy.

00:30:51.950 --> 00:30:57.490
 And that's the thing that makes people feel like their economic needs are being met.

00:30:57.490 --> 00:31:08.560
 And so I think that the importance of story right now and how to tell the kind of stories that work is starting with listening to people.

00:31:08.560 --> 00:31:14.700
 Why would there be an entire group of people at the very top levels of this government who are attracted to alternative facts?

00:31:14.700 --> 00:31:16.210
 What was happening there?

00:31:16.210 --> 00:31:17.120
 What was happening there?

00:31:17.120 --> 00:31:19.590
 And like it's starting with questions like that.

00:31:19.590 --> 00:31:30.190
 We can't invalidate it at this point, which is one of the incredible things that's happened with this recent election is that there is no way, no way to invalidate what's happening right now.

00:31:30.190 --> 00:31:31.190
 It's in our face.

00:31:31.190 --> 00:31:36.190
 It's everyday things are going to break and they're going to break badly if we don't attend to them.

00:31:36.190 --> 00:31:51.600
 So this question about finding a story that people see themselves in that inspires courage to be something bigger, wholer and more connected, even though that leads to more suffering because there is no way to love without suffering.

00:31:51.600 --> 00:31:54.080
 That in and of itself is terrifying.

00:31:54.080 --> 00:31:55.210
 Just that concept.

00:31:55.210 --> 00:31:56.210
 But it's true.

00:31:56.210 --> 00:31:59.230
 Fear is much easier because it leads to disconnection.

00:31:59.230 --> 00:32:12.830
 And then that that that that awful feeling of losing something that you love doesn't have to be dealt with when fear and control and power games are top of mind.

00:32:12.830 --> 00:32:18.140
 So really stories that breed connection and inspire people to have courage.

00:32:18.140 --> 00:32:20.230
 Those feel like the top stories for me.

00:32:20.230 --> 00:32:21.800
 Wonderful.

00:32:21.800 --> 00:32:22.800
 Thanks so much, Nikki.

00:32:22.800 --> 00:32:25.800
 I think that's a really good place to leave it.

00:32:25.800 --> 00:32:30.150
 I just want to make sure we get in, you know, how people can follow along with you.

00:32:30.150 --> 00:32:37.100
 Say the name of your firm again, since you have a new name and say where people can follow along with you and get in touch with you.

00:32:37.100 --> 00:32:40.170
 Soilandshadow.com is the website.

00:32:40.170 --> 00:32:47.540
 And Nikki Silvestri.com is probably the best one to go to right now because the Soil and Shadow website is in process.

00:32:47.540 --> 00:32:59.690
 And following along in real time is my Twitter account, at Nikki, N-I-K-K-I-C Silvestri, and Instagram at Nikki underscore Silvestri.

00:32:59.690 --> 00:33:04.300
 Yeah, and we'll put links to those all in the show notes for this episode.

00:33:04.300 --> 00:33:05.360
 Great.

00:33:05.360 --> 00:33:06.360
 Hey, I really appreciate it, Nikki.

00:33:06.360 --> 00:33:09.360
 Thanks so much for taking the time again to talk to me.

00:33:09.360 --> 00:33:10.360
 Thanks, Devin.

